Scope: .swarm/manifests/S5.7.files. No repo files left modified (verified
`git status --porcelain` byte-identical to the pre-attack snapshot, including
the mtimes I temporarily touched on accounts.html and the harness JS, which
were restored to their original values via `touch -d`). go1.26.3 from
/home/darrell/go-sdk/go, same version the worker cited.

Mechanism claims re-derived from cmd/go/internal/test/test.go on this GOROOT,
all confirmed TRUE:
- `case "getenv"` (line 2032-2033) hashes the value with no directory/root
  guard, unlike `case "stat"`/`case "open"` (lines 2037-2054), which both
  `break` via `if a.Package.Root == "" || search.InDir(name, a.Package.Root)
  == "" { break }` before hashing anything outside the module root.
- os/exec/lp_unix.go:60 `LookPath` calls `os.Getenv("PATH")`, and
  os/env.go:102/113 route that through `testlog.Getenv`. Chain confirmed on
  this platform (linux/amd64).

Empirically re-verified with a controlled experiment (constructed PATH from
scratch directories only, no repo edits, no system package installs):
- Node absent, `PATH` unchanged after `go test` populated the cache -> second
  run reports `(cached) PASS` with `which node` genuinely failing (exit 1).
  Confirms the caveat's core claim.
- `go clean -testcache` and `-count=1` both correctly force a real re-run and
  correctly FAIL when node is absent -> the stated remedies work.
- Touching (not editing) accounts.html's mtime alone (md5 unchanged) busts
  the cache to a real re-run; same for testdata/js/warnings_dom_harness.js
  individually. Both file-read claims hold.
- Opt-out (`BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS=1`, node absent, `-count=1`) produces a
  clean t.Skip, not a failure. No regression there.
- Guard fires correctly with node absent and no cache in play (plain FAIL,
  correct message). No regression there.

FALSIFIED CLAIM (present in both the test-file comment and README.md, so
this is not a comment/README mismatch -- it is the same overclaim in both
places):

  Comment (warnings_client_regression_test.go):
    "installing or removing node, which is what normally changes PATH,
     busts the cache and forces a real re-run"
  ... and, describing the exception:
    "A cached green in that one narrow case is not evidence the harness
     ran"

  README.md (lines 645-647):
    "installing or uninstalling node normally changes PATH, which is what
     busts the cache in the common case"

Both instances frame the PATH-unchanged hole as an edge case bounded by the
two examples given in the comment (an nvm prune, a Docker base image). It is
not narrow: the single most common Linux installation path for node --
apt/dnf-style system package manager -- installs straight into a directory
that is unconditionally on PATH by default and does not touch PATH at all.
Verified directly, not asserted:

  $ apt-get download nodejs   # Ubuntu noble universe, 18.19.1+dfsg-6ubuntu5
  $ dpkg-deb --contents nodejs_18.19.1+dfsg-6ubuntu5_amd64.deb | grep bin/node
  -rwxr-xr-x root/root  26704 2024-04-18 15:08 ./usr/bin/node

  $ echo "$PATH" | tr ':' '\n' | grep -x /usr/bin
  /usr/bin

`/usr/bin` is on PATH unconditionally (via /etc/environment, not the shell
profile the comment worries about), before and after `apt install`/`apt
remove nodejs`. Reproduced the mechanics of exactly this configuration with a
scratch PATH entry acting as the pre-existing "/usr/bin": ran the test to a
genuine PASS with node present in that single directory, removed node from
that same directory (the directory itself never leaves PATH, exactly the
apt-remove case), reran without `go clean -testcache`, and got `(cached)
PASS` with node objectively absent -- the identical hole the comment
attributes only to nvm/Docker.

So "normally changes PATH" is false for the majority real-world install
method on Debian/Ubuntu/RPM-based distros (all install to a fixed,
already-on-PATH system directory), not just for two named edge cases, and
"one narrow case" undersells how reachable the exception is. This is the
same defect class the task flagged for attempts 5 and 6 (miscalibrated
scope of a claim about this cache hole), now manifesting as understatement
rather than overstatement, and it appears in both the comment and the
README, not just one of them.

Not independently re-verified this round (read-only constraint, no repo
edits permitted): the pre-S5 template regression (md5
fb2a5b02af41e835bbe1fc52fd22ed69) and the subtler partial-revert case, since
